&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Systems today-whether human, technological, or ecological-must sustain coherence while facing paradoxes of stability and change, efficiency and adaptability, and order and disorder. Existing research in paradox, resilience, dynamic capabilities, and institutional theory has illuminated aspects of these challenges, yet balance itself is usually assumed rather than theorized directly.&nbsp; 
<div>
 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;This paper develops Whitetropy as a step toward a general theory of dynamic balance. Whitetropy conceptualizes balance as a recursive process that sustains coherence amid entropy (disorder, fragmentation) and syntropy (order, integration). Four interdependent mechanisms-pause, reflection, reframing, and regeneration-form the cycle through which systems continually recalibrate. These mechanisms operate across levels: individuals and AI agents at the micro level, organizations and infrastructures at the meso level, and societies and ecosystems at the macro level.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;By foregrounding balance, Whitetropy reframes paradox not merely as coexistence, resilience not merely as recovery, and dynamic capabilities not merely as reconfiguration. It integrates insights across literatures into a unifying framework that clarifies outcomes such as creativity, resilience, legitimacy, and sustainability, while also specifying boundary conditions such as feedback quality, slack resources, and pluralism.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Whitetropy opens avenues for empirical testing, comparative study, and interdisciplinary dialogue on how balance is maintained across human, technological, and ecological systems. In doing so, it positions balance as a central systemic principle and advances the pursuit of a general theory of dynamic balance in paradox-rich environments.&nbsp;
</div>
